Le Morne is the most striking corner of Mauritius: a basalt mountain rising straight out of a turquoise lagoon. It is also a place of memory inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List. Here is a way to see it in a day.

## Morning: the lagoon by boat

Start early in La Gaulette just north of the peninsula with a boat trip across the lagoon. Most trips stop at [Île aux Bénitiers](/en/places/ile-aux-benitiers) (a coral islet whose beaches face the mountain) and the [Crystal Rock](/en/places/crystal-rock) (a fossil coral rock rising from crystal clear shallows). The water is calmest and clearest in the morning.

Prefer to stay on land? Climb the lower slopes of [Le Morne Brabant](/en/places/le-morne-brabant) instead before the heat. Check the current access rules for the upper section first.

## Midday: the story of the mountain

At the foot of the mountain the [Slave Route Monument](/en/places/slave-route-monument) honours the people who escaped slavery and found refuge on Le Morne. Take time to read the inscriptions: they explain why this mountain matters so much to Mauritians.

## Afternoon: two beaches

Next to the monument [Le Morne public beach](/en/places/le-morne-public-beach) has fine white sand and clear shallow water with the mountain rising behind. On windy days you can watch the kitesurfers further along the lagoon.

For something quieter drive round to the south side of the peninsula to [La Prairie](/en/places/la-prairie-beach). It is a long grassy public beach where Mauritian families picnic at weekends.

## Evening

Stay on the west side of the peninsula for the sunset over the open sea or drive up the hill towards Chamarel: the viewpoint on the way looks down over the whole lagoon in the evening light.

## Good to know

- There is little shade on the beaches so bring a parasol and water and sun protection.
- Le Morne is roughly an hour and a quarter by car from the international airport and about half an hour from Tamarin.
